PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The panel has solidified around 26 states as the minimal strong coalition under an adversarial analysis of the Constitution. Controlling the federal government enables the coalition to admit new states under Article IV, Section 3, expanding the denominator to achieve the required Article V supermajorities without relying on the static 38-state threshold. To pass enabling legislation within 51 months, the coalition must win the presidency and the House by the January 2005 inauguration, while 26 states are strictly required to supply 52 Senators to guarantee the 51-member constitutional quorum under Article I, Section 5 without relying on the Vice President or coercive attendance. Counter-proposals of 34 and 39 states are resolved because the prompt's unanimity condition inherently includes sitting Senators as inhabitants of their states, and offensive state admission prevents defensive dilution.
}

